ggml-cpu: run the value asserts only with GGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERTS
Fold-into: upstream/ggml-cpu

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

A debug build of ggml-cpu (without NDEBUG) examines the data values of some
ops with assert():

- No NaN and no Inf in the results of GELU, GELU_ERF, GELU_QUICK, SILU,
  SILU_BACK, REGLU, GEGLU, SWIGLU, SWIGLU_OAI, GEGLU_ERF and GEGLU_QUICK
- A positive scale in RMS_NORM
- No NaN after the mask, a positive sum and a finite result in SOFT_MAX
- No NaN in the inputs and a finite result in SOFT_MAX_BACK,
  CROSS_ENTROPY_LOSS and CROSS_ENTROPY_LOSS_BACK, and a positive sum in the
  last of these three ops
- A positive sum in the backward op of flash attention
- A finite sum in ggml_vec_dot_f16.

These values come from the input data and not from the code. Thus an input
with a NaN, an Inf or a value that is too large for f16 stops a debug build,
and a release build of the same code gives a result.

This patch adds the macro GGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERT(x) to vec.h. It is assert(x)
when GGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERTS is defined, and it does nothing when that macro is
not defined. Each value assert above uses the macro. The asserts of shapes,
strides and types do not change. Thus a debug build accepts non-finite inputs
as a release build does. To enable the value asserts, add
-DGGML_CPU_VALUE_ASSERTS to the compiler flags of a debug build.

